Record patterns — destructuring.

A type pattern tells you what an object is, and hands you the whole thing. A record pattern, which is final in Java twenty-one, goes one step further. It looks inside a record and binds its components directly, in one move. Instead of matching a point and then calling p dot x and p dot y, you pull x and y straight out of the pattern.

Compare the two. With a type pattern, you write, if obj is an instance of point p, and then inside, you read p dot x plus p dot y. With a record pattern, you write, if obj is an instance of point, open paren, int x, int y — and now x and y are in scope directly, already the right types, so you just add x plus y. That "point of x and y" is the record pattern. It matches when obj is a point, and simultaneously binds x and y to its components. The shape mirrors how the record was declared, so it reads naturally. And you can write var for a component's type and let it be inferred — point of var x, var y.

The real power shows when records contain records. A record pattern can nest, destructuring several layers in a single pattern. Say a point has an x and a y, and a line has a start point and an end point. You can write, if obj is an instance of line, of point var x-one, y-one, and point var x-two, y-two. One pattern reaches through the line, into both points, and binds all four integers at once. Then you compute the length from them. Doing that by hand would be a cascade of casts and accessor calls. The pattern just states the shape you expect, and binds every piece.

Where does this pay off most? In a switch over sealed records. Combine the three ideas from this whole module — a sealed family, a pattern switch, and record patterns — and you get remarkably clear code that the compiler still proves exhaustive. Take a sealed shape, permitting circle and rectangle, each a record. You switch on the shape: case circle of var r, arrow, pi r squared; case rectangle of var w, var h, arrow, w times h. No default, because it's sealed and exhaustive. Each arm names the variant and unpacks its data in a single line. This is the payoff the entire module has been building toward: model the domain as a sealed family of records, then take it apart with a pattern-matching switch — declarative, exhaustive, and checked.

And record patterns compose with when guards too — case circle of var r, when r is greater than ten, for "big circle". The way to read any pattern is as a shape you're asserting: if this value is a line of two points, name their coordinates x-one, y-one, x-two, y-two. If the shape matches, the names are bound; if not, the arm is skipped. That's the very same idea as the instanceof type pattern you started with, now able to see structure, not just type — the destructuring counterpart to how you build these values with a constructor.
